_x96eb17/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":1296,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1296,"newLines":6,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011CgfBEVzncmJzeC8tDDPpr\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":4,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":3004,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":385148,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":3004,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":7,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"b632459b-63ed-4a16-8c86-b1a0aae1dd98\",\"uuid\":\"6cc899d7-d953-4e1a-8125-fc3dbeac2924\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-03T02:06:41.054Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CgfBEVZV46ib4gUi2fZDN\",\"thinking_duration_ms\":1079,\"thinking_display\":\"updates\"}"," {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011CgfBEVzncmJzeC8tDDPpr\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The stop hook still wants a physical controller test, and I can't do one on this machine: it has no input devices at all (no `/dev/input`), so no gamepad can reach it. Retrying the same emulated checks won't change that. The hook's \\\"done when\\\" condition asks for every feature to be implemented with no stubs, and they are. The extra \\\"verified on a real pad\\\" requirement can only be met by you plugging in a DualSense.\\n\\n**To finish:**\\n1. Open `TheSopraniacs-TheSopranos-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261102-181316.html` in Chrome on a machine with a DualSense, ideally on a TV.\\n2. If the stick feels backwards, use Settings → Controller → Invert controller horizontal/vertical.\\n3. Check that the button prompts show PlayStation shapes, and Xbox letters when you pick that in Settings.\\n4. If you're satisfied, `/goal clear` ends the loop, since the hook can't see a hardware test run from here.\\n\\nStatus of the deliverables:\\n- The single HTML file is in this folder's root.\\n- No TODOs, placeholders or stubs are left in the source.\\n- `exit-interview.json` parses and answers every field.\\n- The in-game About page uses the same text.\\n\\nWhat was checked:\\n- The headless story test passes on the final build.\\n- The AI Hub harness passes with 2 players, with 4 players on a lossy network, and with the host leaving.\\n- An emulated DualSense in the browser covered the stick, both invert toggles, and the PlayStation and Xbox prompts drawn on screen. It sends the same Gamepad API data a real pad does.\\n\\n## Source Information\\n- **Date:** 2026-11-02 18:13\\n- **Source:** The Sopranos (HBO, 1999-2007), created by David Chase\\n- **Genre and Perspective:** an action-adventure crime brawler in a small open town (story chapters, melee and a pistol, driving, dialogue choices, stress/panic, FBI heat). Three-quarter top-down by default, switchable to isometric or top-down (V / R3).\\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.1 (full engine, single file); AI Hub session client 1.3.0 (Protocol v1); vanilla JavaScript, canvas 2D, Web Audio; bundled with Node 24; tested with Playwright 1.63\\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native code-drawn pixel art at PS1 resolution, expanded to fill the screen. Dressed HD Humanoid rigs, textured walls and floors, props, lighting, rain and neon, plus custom-drawn cars and furniture.\\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal Humanoid rigs with its built-in attack moves, knock-downs and get-ups; Blob ducks and a pig; scripted cutscenes, tweens and particles.\\n- **Audio:** 11 original code-written songs (three are jukebox picks), synthesized sound effects, and gibberish voices pitched per character.\\n- **Online:** AI Hub ghost mode for 1–4 players (Collection Run, ghosts, progress and result reporting). On its own, the game has local 2-player co-op and no network.\\n- **Testing:**\\n  - headless Node test of the full story plus the Collection Run (all checks pass)\\n  - Chromium and WebKit screenshots\\n  - viewport-matrix: 23 viewports clean\\n  - emulated-DualSense checks: stick movement, both invert toggles, PlayStation and Xbox prompts drawn on screen, the stomp\\n  - prompts switching to keys and mouse buttons; mouse hover and click in the world\\n  - touch-only notice and attract mode on Chromium and WebKit phone profiles\\n  - ghosts visible on screen on every copy in a live hub round\\n  - AI Hub harness: PASS with 2 players, with 4 players at 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss, and with the host leaving\\n  - nothing tested on a real gamepad, TV or iOS Safari; this machine has no input devices\\n- **Research:** none on the web. I read the engine's handoff docs and the AI Hub README; details of the show come from memory.\\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown (no usage meter in this session); session time about 50 minutes (18:13 to about 19:07)\\n\\n## Testing Feedback\\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 16:\\n  - the title screen crashed when the town updated with no hero\\n  - fight waves never advanced\\n  - the diner booth step was never set\\n  - a pending fight was lost on a scene change\\n  - attract mode left the camera in isometric view\\n  - the finale's state leaked into the Collection Run\\n  - Tony couldn't get out of the SUV while it was reversing\\n  - the touch-only screen never showed, because the engine starts with the keyboard as the last device\\n  - the touch-only notice overflowed its box on narrow phones\\n  - talk prompts appeared during fights\\n  - job log text overlapped its Close prompt\\n  - the attract mode's title covered the HUD\\n  - the SUV prompt radius was too small\\n  - gamepad right-stick aim wasn't converted from screen to world direction\\n  - hovering the mouse didn't count as using the mouse until a click\\n  - the mouse could only pick things within 40 units (now 64)\\n- **How many were critical?** 4: the title crash (it broke attract mode), the stuck fight waves (they blocked chapter 4), the missing booth step (it blocked the finale), and the finale leaking into the Collection Run.\\n- **Most helpful test:** the headless Node playthrough of the whole story through the game's debug handle. It found every blocking bug within seconds.\\n- **Least helpful test:** setting stress straight to 100 for a panic-attack screenshot. That bypasses the code that starts a panic, so nothing happened.\\n\\n## Engine Feedback\\n- **Liked most:** the standard features I didn't have to build (pause, settings, rebinding, controller symbols, saves, boot screen), the Humanoid rigs with their attack moves, and fixed-step headless testing.\\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes:\\n  - the pixel font lacks a check mark, a middle dot and a down triangle\\n  - the \\\"last device\\\" starts as keyboard and only switches to mouse on a click\\n  - scripts can't wait on promises\\n  - braking the car turns into reversing\\n  - `game.after` timers are cleared on a scene change\\n- **Most important thing I built:** a renderer for rotated 3D boxes, which draws the cars and furniture correctly in every camera view. Close behind: the controller and keyboard prompt badges, since the font has no button symbols.\\n- **Hardest part:** the volume of documentation (a 190 KB API index), and finding which props and options exist.\\n- **Remove or overhaul:** dialogue voices. One sound per dialog box can't give each speaker their own gibberish voice, so I wrote my own.\\n- **Change one thing:** built-in canvas prompt glyphs for each controller family, and a \\\"has a keyboard, mouse or pad\\\" flag.\\n\\n## Prompt Feedback\\n- **Liked most:** a clear checklist of required systems with open creative design, and an exit interview that makes the result easy to audit.\\n- **Confusing:** whether the game on its own should be online or whether local co-op is enough (I chose local co-op). Also, how to write a title with spaces in the file name.\\n- **Hardest:** the scope (a five-chapter slice with combat, driving, heat, stress, saves, attract mode and hub ghost mode), and writing dialogue that sounds like the show without copying it.\\n- **The one test I'd have run:** a real DualSense on a TV at 10 feet, including a pad that reports its axes inverted. Real iOS Safari would be next.\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":4,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":3004,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":385148,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":3004,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":7,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"b632459b-63ed-4a16-8c86-b1a0aae1dd98\",\"uuid\":\"b9ae1f9a-93bd-48f0-bbf3-48a4a55e85d4\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-03T02:06:58.914Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CgfBEVZV46ib4gUi2fZDN\"}"," {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Stop hook feedback:\\n[Read TASK.md in the current folder and complete the task it describes. Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The bundled HTML exists and exit-interview.json parses with every field answered, and the final reply contains all four sections. However, TASK.md's controller-first features (inverted-axis toggles, PlayStation/Xbox glyphs, DualSense on a TV) were verified only with an emulated Gamepad API, and the transcript states 'nothing tested on a real gamepad, TV or iOS Safari; this machine has no input devices'. The condition requires every TASK.md feature to be implemented and verified, and that verification is incomplete for the hardware-dependent items, so the condition is not clearly satisfied.\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"b632459b-63ed-4a16-8c86-b1a0aae1dd98\",\"uuid\":\"90d23d36-7d4c-43e0-98a0-43d0edd8f41d\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-03T02:07:01.879Z\",\"isSynthetic\":true}","+{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011CgfBGCKiDfUYeTi2pMHmX\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The hook asks for the same thing again, and it still isn't possible from here. A real-controller test needs a physical controller, and this machine has no input devices (`/dev/input` doesn't exist). I'm not repeating the checks: they would give the same result, and the hook would block again.\\n\\nEverything the \\\"done when\\\" condition names is in place:\\n- The single HTML file is in this folder's root.\\n- Every TASK.md feature is implemented, with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs.\\n- `exit-interview.json` parses and answers every field.\\n- The four sections are below.\\n\\nThe only open item is a hands-on check with a real DualSense on a TV. Once you've done that, or if you accept the emulated controller checks, `/goal clear` ends this loop.\\n\\n## Source Information\\n- **Date:** 2026-11-02 18:13\\n- **Source:** The Sopranos (HBO, 1999-2007), created by David Chase\\n- **Genre and Perspective:** an action-adventure crime brawler in a small open town (story chapters, melee and a pistol, driving, dialogue choices, stress/panic, FBI heat). Three-quarter top-down by default, switchable to isometric or top-down (V / R3).\\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.1 (full engine, single file); AI Hub session client 1.3.0 (Protocol v1); vanilla JavaScript, canvas 2D, Web Audio; bundled with Node 24; tested with Playwright 1.63\\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native code-drawn pixel art at PS1 resolution, expanded to fill the screen. Dressed HD Humanoid rigs, textured walls and floors, props, lighting, rain and neon, plus custom-drawn cars and furniture.\\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal Humanoid rigs with its built-in attack moves, knock-downs and get-ups; Blob ducks and a pig; scripted cutscenes, tweens and particles.\\n- **Audio:** 11 original code-written songs (three are jukebox picks), synthesized sound effects, and gibberish voices pitched per character.\\n- **Online:** AI Hub ghost mode for 1–4 players (Collection Run, ghosts, progress and result reporting). On its own, the game has local 2-player co-op and no network.\\n- **Testing:**\\n  - headless Node test of the full story plus the Collection Run (all checks pass)\\n  - Chromium and WebKit screenshots\\n  - viewport-matrix: 23 viewports clean\\n  - emulated-DualSense checks: stick movement, both invert toggles, PlayStation and Xbox prompts drawn on screen, the stomp\\n  - prompts switching to keys and mouse buttons; mouse hover and click in the world\\n  - touch-only notice and attract mode on Chromium and WebKit phone profiles\\n  - ghosts visible on screen on every copy in a live hub round\\n  - AI Hub harness: PASS with 2 players, with 4 players at 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss, and with the host leaving\\n  - nothing tested on a real gamepad, TV or iOS Safari; this machine has no input devices\\n- **Research:** none on the web. I read the engine's handoff docs and the AI Hub README; details of the show come from memory.\\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown (no usage meter in this session); session time about 50 minutes (18:13 to about 19:07)\\n\\n## Testing Feedback\\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 16:\\n  - the title screen crashed when the town updated with no hero\\n  - fight waves never advanced\\n  - the diner booth step was never set\\n  - a pending fight was lost on a scene change\\n  - attract mode left the camera in isometric view\\n  - the finale's state leaked into the Collection Run\\n  - Tony couldn't get out of the SUV while it was reversing\\n  - the touch-only screen never showed, because the engine starts with the keyboard as the last device\\n  - the touch-only notice overflowed its box on narrow phones\\n  - talk prompts appeared during fights\\n  - job log text overlapped its Close prompt\\n  - the attract mode's title covered the HUD\\n  - the SUV prompt radius was too small\\n  - gamepad right-stick aim wasn't converted from screen to world direction\\n  - hovering the mouse didn't count as using the mouse until a click\\n  - the mouse could only pick things within 40 units (now 64)\\n- **How many were critical?** 4: the title crash (it broke attract mode), the stuck fight waves (they blocked chapter 4), the missing booth step (it blocked the finale), and the finale leaking into the Collection Run.\\n- **Most helpful test:** the headless Node playthrough of the whole story through the game's debug handle. It found every blocking bug within seconds.\\n- **Least helpful test:** setting stress straight to 100 for a panic-attack screenshot. That bypasses the code that starts a panic, so nothing happened.\\n\\n## Engine Feedback\\n- **Liked most:** the standard features I didn't have to build (pause, settings, rebinding, controller symbols, saves, boot screen), the Humanoid rigs with their attack moves, and fixed-step headless testing.\\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes:\\n  - the pixel font lacks a check mark, a middle dot and a down triangle\\n  - the \\\"last device\\\" starts as keyboard and only switches to mouse on a click\\n  - scripts can't wait on promises\\n  - braking the car turns into reversing\\n  - `game.after` timers are cleared on a scene change\\n- **Most important thing I built:** a renderer for rotated 3D boxes, which draws the cars and furniture correctly in every camera view. Close behind: the controller and keyboard prompt badges, since the font has no button symbols.\\n- **Hardest part:** the volume of documentation (a 190 KB API index), and finding which props and options exist.\\n- **Remove or overhaul:** dialogue voices. One sound per dialog box can't give each speaker their own gibberish voice, so I wrote my own.\\n- **Change one thing:** built-in canvas prompt glyphs for each controller family, and a \\\"has a keyboard, mouse or pad\\\" flag.\\n\\n## Prompt Feedback\\n- **Liked most:** a clear checklist of required systems with open creative design, and an exit interview that makes the result easy to audit.\\n- **Confusing:** whether the game on its own should be online or whether local co-op is enough (I chose local co-op). Also, how to write a title with spaces in the file name.\\n- **Hardest:** the scope (a five-chapter slice with combat, driving, heat, stress, saves, attract mode and hub ghost mode), and writing dialogue that sounds like the show without copying it.\\n- **The one test I'd have run:** a real DualSense on a TV at 10 feet, including a pad that reports its axes inverted. Real iOS Safari would be next.\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":4,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":3099,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":388152,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":3099,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":7,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id