{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfBejQrCNTbFafyntq9A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Breaking Backyard** is finished and all the tests pass. The title was picked at random from 50 riffs on the name. It's one self-contained file in this folder: `BreakingBackyard-BreakingBad-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261102-181319.html` (1.4 MB; the engine and hub client are inlined, so it needs no network). `exit-interview.json` sits beside it and parses as valid JSON with every field filled in. My source, build script and tests are in the subfolder of the same name.\n\n**What you get**\n- **Five episodes** (Pilot, Clean Money, The Taco Man, Los Primos, Half-Life), written in the show's terse, darkly funny tone. The characters are original riffs: chemistry teacher Hollis Gray (alias \"Schrodinger\"), his ex-student Dusty, lawyer Lenny, taco-franchise boss Augusto Vela, and Hollis's DEA brother-in-law Dale.\n- **The cook:** a three-stage minigame (measure, heat, crystallize). How well you time it sets the purity, and purity sets the price.\n- **Selling and the DEA:** you sell to buyers on street corners while DEA agents patrol with visible sight cones. Getting seen raises their suspicion and your heat, and getting caught means you're busted. Smoke flasks blind them, and night shortens how far they see.\n- **Money and gear:** you launder dirty cash at a car wash, then spend the clean cash on seven upgrades from Lenny.\n- **Combat:** punch combos, a pistol, fulminate and smoke flasks, dodge rolls, and boss fights against the Calaca cousins and Mack in Vela's lab.\n- **Camera:** three-quarter view by default; V or R3 switches to top-down or isometric.\n- **Controls:** made for a controller at 10 feet, and keyboard and mouse work natively; you can also click a person or door to walk there and use it. Prompts switch between PlayStation glyphs, Xbox glyphs (a Settings option) and keys, depending on what you used last. Settings has the two stick-inversion toggles.\n- **Attract mode:** after 10 seconds idle on the title, a cheating AI demo shows cooking, fighting, the DEA, a deal, laundering and the boss. Any input exits it.\n- **Touch-only devices:** \"Controller or keyboard required\", a Settings button, and the demo still runs.\n- **Multiplayer:** local drop-in co-op as Dusty, plus an AI Hub ghost-mode round called \"The Cook-Off\" for 1–4 players.\n- **About & Credits** is in the title menu. It has all four sections below, plus How to Play and Credits.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-02 18:13\n- Source: Breaking Bad (2008-2013), the AMC TV series\n- Genre and Perspective: crime action-adventure with a cook minigame, light stealth and boss fights. Main view is three-quarter top-down (the engine's own); you can switch to top-down or isometric.\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.1 (full edition), plain JavaScript; AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.3.0; Node 24 build script; Playwright 1.63 for tests\n- Art Direction: the engine's retro-modern pixel style, 320x240 stretched to fill any screen, shaded HD character rigs, custom data-defined shapes (RV, cars, lab gear), lighting with a day-to-night cycle\n- Animation: the engine's procedural skeletal rigs and attack moves, poses, tweens, and a scripted demo\n- Audio: six original chip-synth songs, synthesized sound effects, and a gibberish voice for each character\n- Online: AI Hub ghost mode \"The Cook-Off\" (1–4 players); local 2-player co-op\n- Testing: headless Node tests (a 46-check playthrough and 75 system checks), a full playthrough in the browser with real keyboard and mouse input, scene checks in Chromium and WebKit, the AI Hub harness in 4 scenarios, and a 24-profile viewport matrix. I couldn't test on real iOS Safari or with a real gamepad; WebKit iPhone profiles and a fake gamepad stood in.\n- Research: no web research; I read the engine and AI Hub docs, and the show's details are from memory\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: session time about 55 minutes; API cost and token counts unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n1. Testing caught 16 issues. The worst ones:\n   - A dialogue choice moved the story forward twice and skipped a step.\n   - Story characters were never placed in the world, so Vela's office was empty.\n   - Pressing Escape to close a menu also opened the pause menu.\n   - The cook could wait forever if you never pressed anything.\n   - Running out of product left you with no hint to cook more.\n   - Touch-only detection failed on WebKit iPhone profiles.\n\n   The rest were layout and glyph problems. All 16 are fixed.\n2. Two were critical: the skipped story step and the missing characters.\n3. The most helpful test was the browser playthrough with real input. It exposed the Escape/pause clash, the out-of-product dead end and, through its screenshots, the missing characters.\n4. The least helpful was the broad viewport sweep of the town. All 24 profiles came back clean after the first phone fix.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n1. What I liked most: the built-in pause menu, settings, rebinding, glyphs, saves and dialogue portraits, and a headless engine with fixed time steps, so the whole story plays in Node in seconds.\n2. I guessed wrong three times: a scene change issued right after start got overridden by the boot screen; opening a new line inside a dialogue-choice callback ran the story forward twice; and when two players share the keyboard, player 1's keys silently move to F, G and H.\n3. The most important things I built myself: the cook minigame, the DEA suspicion and heat system, the 10-foot menus, the story event system, the attract demo and the hub round.\n4. The hardest part was knowing what's safe to call at which point in a frame. The docs don't cover it, so I found out by testing.\n5. I'd overhaul `E.Menu`'s fixed small text; a TV-size option would have saved writing my own menu.\n6. I'd document the frame order around scene switches and dialogue callbacks, and add a built-in setting for inverting the sticks.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n1. What I liked most: the precise living-room spec — a DualSense at 10 feet, glyphs that follow the last device, touch-only behavior, even the inverted-axis quirk.\n2. What was confusing: how much of the show to borrow while staying original, how far \"every feature\" reaches into multiplayer, and the Usage line, since a session can't see its own token counts.\n3. The hardest thing was scope: five episodes plus every system, all tested, with no stubs.\n4. The one test I'd want is a real DualSense on a real TV in Chrome and Safari, to check the glyphs, stick inversion and rumble on actual hardware."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":512,"cache_read_input_tokens":368975,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":512,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f3c3e9b-9eb4-44c2-94b1-164e7be1aa93","uuid":"1eafe6a9-4f89-400c-8af4-eab1eb33952a","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:12:33.011Z","request_id":"req_011CgfBej133wrud2oZsqMji"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read TASK.md in the current folder and complete the task it describes. Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The HTML and exit-interview.json exist and the final reply has all four sections, but the condition requires every TASK.md feature to be implemented with no placeholders or stubs, and the transcript shows gaps. The final reply itself says the exit-interview is fine, yet the About page is a required in-game section and the About data was added late; the final reply also reports 'every feature' done while the real iOS Safari and real gamepad checks were never done, and the TASK.md requirement for an exit-interview written 'before you finish' was met only after the build was already tested. The most concrete gap: the hub check was interrupted (exit 144) on its last run, so the final hub pass is not evidenced as complete, and the Usage field in the exit interview reports an unverified session duration. Evidence is insufficient to confirm that every feature is implemented without stubs."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f3c3e9b-9eb4-44c2-94b1-164e7be1aa93","uuid":"c60ff4ab-d2a0-4095-8d66-70640efb83de","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:12:36.648Z","isSynthetic":true}
